{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nAgents sometimes botch the `rebar-ticket:` trailer, leaving a ticket's change unlinkable at close time. The `COMMITS` event type and `attach_commits` reducer already exist (`rebar.attach_commits`, defined at `src/rebar/_lib_writes.py:697` and re-exported from `src/rebar/__init__.py`) — used by the code-review workflow. What is missing is an author-facing repair surface and a consumer. Approved design (analysis ticket b902-18af-14ed-4da1): expose retroactive commit linking on ALL THREE surfaces (CLI, Python library, MCP), document it clearly, and then add a cheap deterministic close-gate check that the linked change's diff is consistent with the recorded file_impact. Explicitly OUT of scope: using the linked commits as a completion-verifier diff basis — linked commits may be incomplete even when they match file_impact, and CV already prefetches from file_impact.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. CLI verb `rebar attach-commits <ticket> <sha>...` wrapping the existing `rebar.attach_commits` seam. SHA validation (each argument resolves to a commit object in the repository, via `git rev-parse --verify <sha>^{commit}`) lives IN the shared seam itself, not the CLI, so the CLI, Python library, and MCP tool all inherit it identically; union-add semantics unchanged. **Validation is ALL-OR-NOTHING**: every supplied SHA is validated BEFORE any event is appended; if one or more fail to resolve, NO `COMMITS` event is recorded and the call raises an error naming every offending SHA. A unit test asserts this explicitly (a mixed valid/invalid batch leaves `state.commits` unchanged). Because `_lib_writes.py` is already 778 LOC against the 800-LOC hard cap, the validation helper lives in the new shared module of step 3, not inline in `_lib_writes.py`.\n2. Library re-export (`rebar.attach_commits` already importable — confirm and document) and MCP tool (`attach_commits`) with outputSchema, following the existing write-tool pattern in `src/rebar/mcp_server.py`.\n3. DET close check, added to the existing completion precheck in `src/rebar/_commands/close_precheck.py` (the module that today raises the \"cannot confirm the work landed\" error) — there is no `src/rebar/workflow/close_gate.py`. Commit-set discovery is exactly the two sources that already exist, unioned: (i) `state.commits` populated by COMMITS events (the attach-commits surface added here), and (ii) the referencing-commit scan the precheck already performs (`_referencing_commit_exists`, `close_precheck.py:94`, commits reachable from the worktree HEAD carrying a `rebar-ticket:` trailer or leading `<id>:` subject for the ticket or a descendant). That existing function returns only a bool, so it is refactored into a SHA-returning `_referencing_commits(...)` with `_referencing_commit_exists` kept as a thin wrapper over it — the documented monkeypatch target and its call sites keep working unchanged. For each discovered commit, collect `git show --name-only` changed paths; a changed path that is neither within the recorded `file_impact` nor matched by this feature's OWN exemption globs (`tests/**`, `docs/**`, `**/*.md`, `CHANGELOG.md` — defined and documented here, not inherited from any other gate) is a deterministic close BLOCK naming the offending commit and paths. **Path-matching semantics are NOT re-derived**: both sides are normalized repo-relative POSIX paths; a `file_impact` entry matches a changed path on exact equality, or as a directory prefix when the entry ends with `/` or names an existing directory; exemption globs are matched with the repo's established `**/`-aware glob helper (the `fnmatch(path, pat) or (pat.startswith(\"**/\") and fnmatch(path, pat[3:]))` semantics currently at `src/rebar/llm/prompting/prompts.py:746`, mirrored in `llm/code_review/registry.py` and `grounding/oracle.py`) so `tests/**` and `**/*.md` match at the repo root as intended. Because the DET check must NOT import `rebar.llm` (that package is the optional `[agents]` extra and this path is deliberately LLM-free), that helper is hoisted into a dependency-neutral shared module `src/rebar/_engine_support/impact_paths.py` holding both matchers, and `prompts._glob_match` becomes a thin delegating wrapper — one implementation, no fourth copy. No substring matching. No LLM. A ticket with no discovered commits keeps today's behavior (the existing cannot-confirm-work-landed error), and a ticket without `file_impact` is out of scope for this check.\n4. **Remedy for a legitimate block** (so the tightened gate has a clear escape path): the block message itself names the offending paths and tells the author to declare them — the fix is `rebar set-file-impact <ticket> …` to amend the ticket's `file_impact` to include the additional path, after which the close proceeds; the pre-existing `--force=\"<reason>\"` override remains the break-glass. This remedy is stated in the block message AND in the docs of step 5.\n5. Documentation: `docs/cli-reference.md`, `docs/mcp-reference.md`, and the commit-trailer guide (`src/rebar/_guides/`, surfaced by `rebar explain commit-trailer`) each name the repair verb, when to use it, and the remedy from step 4.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar attach-commits <ticket> <sha>` records a COMMITS event and `rebar show` reflects it; invalid SHAs are rejected with a clear error naming them, and a batch containing ANY invalid SHA records NOTHING (all-or-nothing). Verify: `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" python -m pytest tests/unit/test_attach_commits.py -x` passes, including a test asserting `state.commits` is unchanged after a mixed valid/invalid batch.\n- [ ] The MCP server exposes `attach_commits` with an outputSchema and the Python library documents the call; both route through the same seam as the CLI. Verify: MCP tool-inventory test updated and passing.\n- [ ] With file_impact recorded and linked commits present, a commit touching a path outside the declared impact deterministically blocks close with the offending paths named; a conforming diff does not block; an exempt path (`tests/**`, `docs/**`, `**/*.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`) does not block, including at the repo root. Verify: unit tests cover all three directions.\n- [ ] The DET check imports no `rebar.llm` module, and the `**/`-aware glob semantics have exactly one implementation. Verify: a test asserts `rebar.llm` is absent from `sys.modules` after running the DET check in a fresh interpreter, and that `prompts._glob_match` delegates to the shared helper.\n- [ ] The block message and the three docs name the verb, the repair workflow, and the `set-file-impact` remedy. Verify: grep assertions in the docs test tier."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786513309339153001,"uuid":"1153d3e5-69c1-490e-a031-ca6fe8ca7f2a"}